From a59807038302fdb1234b5dc9dae27c42b9a7fb60 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: atomozero <bernardi.andrea@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:40:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 010/203] ARM: add a timer driver for the Cortex-A9 private
 timer (TWD)

Cortex-A9 has no generic timer, so on boards without a supported SoC
timer the kernel panicked with 'No hardware timer found!'.

The TWD (arm,cortex-a9-twd-timer) exists on every Cortex-A9 MPCore. It
has a single down counter, used both as clock source (the time is
accumulated from the ticks consumed since the last load) and as event
timer. Its rate (PERIPHCLK) is usually not in the device tree, so the
EFI loader measures it against the firmware's Stall() and passes it in
the new arch_args.timerFrequency.

The loader only falls back to the TWD when no other supported timer was
found, so e.g. Tegra 2 keeps using its own timer.

Tested on QEMU vexpress-a9 (Cortex-A9, GICv1): the timer calibrates to
about 100 MHz and the kernel boots until it looks for the boot volume.
